b.guide(S1, "HCM is the most common genetic cardiovascular disease worldwide and the most common cause of SCD; it is autosomal dominant, with myosin binding protein C ranked above myosin heavy chain (p336). Incidence is M = F, females progress more to HF with poor prognosis and 95–99% are asymptomatic.\nPaediatric mimics are Pompe disease (glycogen storage disease type 2, short PR), Fabry disease, Noonan syndrome and Friedreich ataxia; complications are HF progression, arrhythmias/AF and SCD.\nHCM is LV thickness ≥15 mm without dilatation after excluding other capable cardiac/systemic disease; systemic HTN and AS are the other ≥15 mm causes. Athlete's heart has LV thickness <15 mm (10–12 mm), a large cavity, normal diastolic filling and improvement with rest; HCM has ≥15 mm, a small cavity, abnormal filling and no improvement.")
b.guide(S2, "Asymmetric HCM involves only the left side, septal and anterior wall, sparing apex and posterior wall; hypertrophy is inappropriate because afterload is not raised (p337). LVOT obstruction is present in one third, absent/normal in one third and dynamic in one third.\nYamaguchi disease is a Japanese-population HCM variant with apical hypertrophy and deep/giant T inversions. Diastolic dysfunction runs concentric LVH → smaller cavity → raised LVEDP → raised LAP.\nAtrial stretch/dilatation raises the atrial CO contribution from 25 to 40% and AF risk; raised PCWP produces pulmonary edema and dyspnea, with tachycardia-related exertional dyspnea.")
b.guide(S3, "Asymmetrical septal hypertrophy projects into the LVOT, narrows it and creates a high-velocity jet with low-pressure zones that draw the anterior mitral leaflet into the septum/LVOT; SAM is printed after two thirds of systole (p338).\nMild secondary MR causes a PSM at the apex, while increased LVOT obstruction causes an ESM at the lower left sternal border. A 30 mmHg gradient reflects subaortic/subvalvular obstruction and determines progression to HF (most important) and symptom severity.\nThe AS-location note labels valvular most common, subvalvular as the HCM form and supravalvular/Williams least common; angina with normal coronaries is due to microvascular dysfunction.")
b.guide(S4, "Again 95–99% are asymptomatic (p339). Raised LAP/LVEDP gives exertional dyspnea/fatigue, orthopnea and PND in advanced disease; LVOT obstruction gives angina/syncope/dyspnea; palpitations come from arrhythmias with AF the commonest sustained arrhythmia, and syncope is repeated separately.\nBP and JVP are normal, the pulse is jerky and bisferiens has two systolic peaks with percussion > tidal and the second peak related to SAM obstruction. The double apical impulse is due to SAM and the triple impulse to presystolic accentuation.\nS1 is normal, S2 reverse split from prolonged ejection, S3 is + from reduced filling of a non-compliant ventricle and S4 ++ from a vigorously contracting sinus-rhythm atrium. Murmurs are ESM at LLSB and PSM at apex; ESM intensity follows the LV–aorta gradient and preload, afterload and inotropy.")
b.guide(S5, "HCM prefers high preload, high afterload and low inotropy (p340). Reduced preload and afterload shrink the cavity/LVOT and raise obstruction; increased inotropy draws septum and mitral valve into the LVOT.\nHydration relieves. Venodilators/diuretics, aggressive BP reduction, moderate–severe activity and inotropes such as digoxin aggravate; inotropes are contraindicated for elevating BP in HCM.\nStanding Valsalva, amyl nitrite and isoproterenol increase murmur intensity by lowering preload/afterload or raising contractility; hand grip and phenylephrine reduce it by raising afterload. All murmurs decrease with standing Valsalva except MVP (duration ↑) and HCM (intensity ↑). ECG shows LVH and precordial ST–T changes/deep giant T inversions in Yamaguchi.")
b.guide(S6, "ECHO is the gold standard for HCM and requires ≥15 mm LV thickening without dilatation or another valvular/systemic/cardiac cause; Doppler shows the dagger sign (p341). Medical management is beta blocker or CCB plus disopyramide.\nBeta blocker is DOC; propranolol is printed at 2 mg/kg (max 480 mg), reducing exercise HR response and rest/exercise LVOT gradient, relieving angina by lowering myocardial O₂ demand and increasing diastolic filling. CCBs are used for beta-blocker contraindication/intolerance; named drugs are verapamil and diltiazem.\nICD indications are previous cardiac arrest, family history of SCD, sustained VT, abnormal Holter, unexplained syncope and LVH >30 mm.")
b.guide(S7, "Disopyramide is class Ia, added to beta blocker/CCB if no relief, with QT prolongation, anticholinergic effects and reduced EF (p342). Surgery is for refractory HCM with gradient >50 mmHg despite medical therapy, by septal myomectomy or alcohol septal ablation.\nRCM is introduced as non-genetic, more common than HCM and nested inside diastolic dysfunction. Constrictive pericarditis is the constrictive branch; restrictive physiology and restrictive CMP are the more severe restrictive branch.\nRCM ventricles are stiff/non-compliant (R > L), non-dilated and of normal thickness, raising filling pressure and RAP. Systemic congestion gives JVP, ascites, edema and hepatomegaly; atrial stretch raises AF risk. Systolic function is almost normal, dimensions normal/rarely reduced, atria massively enlarged and RVEDP > LVEDP. Subtle exercise intolerance is first, restrictive physiology is the severest diastolic dysfunction and biventricular diastolic failure is R > L.")
b.guide(S8, "RCM causes are non-infiltrative DM/scleroderma; intracellular iron (hemochromatosis, dilated > RCM), glycosphingolipid (Fabry) and glycogen (Pompe) accumulation (the latter two HCM > RCM); and intercellular amyloidosis, sarcoidosis (DCM > RCM), Loeffler syndrome and tropical endomyocardial fibrosis (p343). The figure labels lamellar bodies and zebra/onion-skin appearance in Fabry disease, with Ascaris lumbricoides printed as the most common associated infection.\nThe mutation recap gives DCMP truncated-variant titin, HCM myosin-binding-protein and RCM amyloidosis.\nAmyloidosis is classified as primary AL, secondary AA (no heart, only kidney) and familial ATTR, with heart involved in all except secondary AA. ATTR is split into wild senile systemic amyloidosis and mutant familial amyloid polyneuropathy; localized amyloidosis produces atrial natriuretic factor and involves the heart. Features are bilateral thickened ventricles, biatrial dilatation, interatrial septal thickening and diastolic > systolic HF.")
b.guide(S9, "Amyloidosis ECG shows low-voltage complexes and a pseudo-infarct pattern; ECHO shows hypertrophy (L > R); cardiac MRI shows a glittering hyperrefractile/sparkling myocardium due to interatrial septal thickening, and prognosis is poor (p344).\nARVD is autosomal recessive with mutated desmosomes, fibrofatty replacement of RV myocardium and arrhythmias/SCD. Tachycardia-induced CMP follows recurrent tachycardia → myocardial-fibre disruption → CMP → AF.\nTakotsubo is also called broken-heart syndrome, transient apical ballooning, neurogenic myocardial stunning, stress-induced CMP and catecholamine-induced CMP. It affects middle-aged females (F >>> M), follows chronic stress/sympathetic overactivity and causes transient LV dysfunction with a hypokinetic ballooned apex (catecholamine-induced transient myocardial stunning).")
b.guide(S10, "Takotsubo ECG can mimic ACS or acute HF; troponin I rises but with a reduced rate of rise, and NT-proBNP rises markedly (p345).\nAngiography shows normal coronaries, a hypokinetic apex and a hypercontractile base; ECHO shows regional wall-motion abnormality not confined to one coronary territory.\nLong-term prognosis is excellent. Short-term complications are shock, LV rupture and thromboembolism, bracketed as the same as MI. Treatment is no specific therapy and carvedilol.")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    b.write()
